// Cantorel strings (en) — motif names + dex entries; dialogue keys
// land with the story scripts.
{
    "dex.burrbass": "It naps deep in hedges, droning a bass line that knots the brush tighter around it. A traveler it trusts can ask instead for the chord that parts the brambles.",
    "dex.buzzoon": "The reed-shaped horn on its abdomen drones a note that wilts orchard pests, so Pausa growers tolerate the smell. The drone sinks as venom builds — field hands know to leave at the low octave.",
    "dex.carilloy": "A drifting frame of scavenged bells that adopts the hour-toll of whatever town it settles over, always a beat behind the true one. Bell-wardens in Cadenza swear their towers ring cleaner when a Carilloy roosts in the rafters.",
    "dex.cellorca": "Its hollow body holds a low open C that carries for miles beneath the surface, and deep-water sailors steer through fog by it. When a Cellorca rises to breathe, the whole bay briefly hums in sympathy.",
    "dex.dirgeist": "A funeral song that was never allowed its final chord, still circling the resolution it is owed. It drifts through empty halls at night, humming the endings of other Motes' phrases a half-step flat.",
    "dex.embaritone": "Its voice drops a full octave at evolution and keeps slipping lower for weeks, to its evident embarrassment. The brass plates along its throat warm an entire room while it runs its evening scales.",
    "dex.fanfyre": "Its mane flares in quick brass figures when it is excited, and lamplighters in Cantorel's old quarters keep one on their rounds instead of a lantern. A startled Fanfyre blurts the same four-note fanfare its line has opened with for centuries.",
    "dex.glissicle": "A rack of hollow icicles that plays a falling scale each time it melts a little, then freezes itself back into tune overnight. In the high passes, a sudden thaw is called losing the choir.",
    "dex.maestroar": "Its roar lands like a downbeat: wild Motes within earshot fall into tempo whether they mean to or not. Conservatory ushers swear the flames of its mane rise and fall in strict four-four.",
    "dex.pipling": "Hatchlings pipe the three notes their parents sang into the egg, badly off-key at first. Walkers on Route 1 set their morning pace to the flocks' practice scales.",
    "dex.rattacca": "It strikes without pause, chaining bites so fast that listeners hear a single rolled note. Pausa's bell-keepers pay it in seed to gnaw rust from the carillon wires.",
    "dex.rippeggio": "It climbs streams by plucking the surface, each hop landing a note higher than the last. Ferry crews judge a pool healthy by how many Rippeggio arpeggios they hear at dusk.",
    "dex.soarprano": "It climbs as it sings, holding the highest note at the top of its loop before diving home. Pausa's bells are retuned twice a year to match the dawn aria from the Route 1 hedgerows.",
    "dex.solfawn": "It learns its scale the way other fawns learn to stand, wobbling up from do to sol by its first week. Herders leave their gates unlatched, since a Solfawn always comes home singing the note it left on.",
    "dex.sonatler": "Its antlers have grown into a full string section, and it plays one long sonata across its lifetime without repeating a movement. Groves where a Sonatler grazes grow back in straight, evenly spaced rows.",
    "dex.tidalegro": "Grown impatient with still water, it bows across harbor swells at a tempo no oarsman can match. Its wake leaves a run of eighth-note eddies that take whole minutes to settle.",
    "dex.timpanite": "Its shell is a slab of layered slate stretched tight as a drumhead, and it booms the downbeat for any work-song within earshot. Quarry crews outside Prelude Town pace their hammers to it.",
    "dex.tremole": "Its fur quivers at the pitch of approaching footsteps, and Pausa farmers read their fields by which way the grass trembles. Cornered, it holds one thin note until the danger walks past.",
    "dex.vinebrato": "The vines strung along its antlers never stop trembling, holding a vibrato even while it sleeps. Brush cut by its bow-stroke falls in neat, mowed rows.",
    "dex.zapresto": "It darts along fence-rails so fast that its static crackle arrives a note after it does. Orchard keepers wrap their gate latches in felt so a passing Zapresto cannot arc to the iron.",
